\pard\plain \fi720\sl288\slmult1\ltrch\loch {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93They might agree about that,\u8221\'94 Mandri admitted, \u8220\'93But the emotional content of the color, the }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 meaning}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  of the color...maybe a better example is the word \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91rain\u8217\'92. Tell a cat that the rain is coming, and it will scream. Tell a farmer, and he will leap for joy.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Don\u8217\'92t be ridiculous,\u8221\'94 Vigram said as his slaves ferried steaming plates of food out of the kitchen. \u8220\'93Both the cat and the farmer agree that \u8216\'91rain\u8217\'92 refers to water from the sky. They may disagree about their }{\f1\fs24\b0\i1 feelings}{\f0\fs24\b0\i0  towards the rain. But the word \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8216\'91rain\u8217\'92 refers to rain. It can only ever refer to rain.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93Not always,\u8221\'94 Mandri said, \u8220\'93But we should put that to the side because I\u8217\'92m talking about something other than what a word points to. I mean, this gets to the fundamental difference here: we\u8217\'92re disagreeing about what \u8216\'91meaning\u8217\'92 means, which, incidentally, proves my argument.\u8221\'94}
\par\plain {\f0\fs24\b0\i0 \loch\af0\hich\af0\dbch\af0\uc1\u8220\'93It only proves you are the spawn of a degenerate culture.\u8221\'94}}